1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,700
Priya Raman: Hi Ruth, it's Priya from Fennick.

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:09,500
Priya Raman: Can you hear me okay?

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,050
Ruth Adeyemi: Yep, I've got you.

4
00:00:12,150 --> 00:00:14,200
Ruth Adeyemi: Sorry, give me one second, I just came in off the floor.

5
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:16,350
Ruth Adeyemi: Okay.

6
00:00:16,450 --> 00:00:18,500
Ruth Adeyemi: Hi.

7
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,200
Priya Raman: No rush at all.

8
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:25,500
Priya Raman: Were you out on the pour deck?

9
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,100
Ruth Adeyemi: I was.

10
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:34,300
Ruth Adeyemi: We had a ladle running a little cold this morning, so I went out to look at it myself.

11
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:38,500
Ruth Adeyemi: It's fine now.

12
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:46,200
Priya Raman: I've only ever watched a pour from behind the yellow line, and I could still feel the heat on my face.

13
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:53,500
Priya Raman: I don't know how your crew does it.

14
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,100
Ruth Adeyemi: You get used to it.

15
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:58,300
Ruth Adeyemi: Mostly.

16
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:00,500
Ruth Adeyemi: In August nobody gets used to anything.

17
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Priya Raman: I believe it.

18
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:11,100
Priya Raman: Well, thanks for making the time on a day like that.

19
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:16,200
Priya Raman: Here's what I was hoping to do with our twenty-five minutes.

20
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:21,300
Priya Raman: I'd like to hear how maintenance actually runs at Harrow Creek today, share a little about what we've seen at other plants like yours, and then figure out together if a second conversation even makes sense.

21
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:26,400
Priya Raman: Does that work?

22
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:31,500
Priya Raman: And is there anything you want to make sure we cover?

23
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,767
Ruth Adeyemi: That works.

24
00:01:36,867 --> 00:01:41,633
Ruth Adeyemi: The main thing for me is whether this works for guys who don't sit at a desk.

25
00:01:41,733 --> 00:01:46,500
Ruth Adeyemi: My crew isn't going to walk back to a computer to log anything.

26
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:49,800
Priya Raman: That's fair.

27
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:52,700
Priya Raman: I'll make sure we get there.

28
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:55,600
Priya Raman: So, walk me through a normal morning.

29
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:58,500
Priya Raman: How does maintenance actually run today?

30
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,550
Ruth Adeyemi: So every morning around five-thirty, our planner prints the work orders for the day.

31
00:02:04,650 --> 00:02:10,200
Ruth Adeyemi: Each tech gets a stack.

32
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:15,850
Ruth Adeyemi: They do the jobs, they write on the paper, and they drop it in a tray by the maintenance office.

33
00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:21,500
Ruth Adeyemi: And then somebody types it in when they get a chance.

34
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,200
Priya Raman: When they get a chance.

35
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:28,500
Priya Raman: How often is that, really?

36
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,050
Ruth Adeyemi: Honestly?

37
00:02:32,150 --> 00:02:35,200
Ruth Adeyemi: Some weeks it's every day.

38
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:38,350
Ruth Adeyemi: Some weeks the tray just fills up and our planner catches up on a Friday.

39
00:02:38,450 --> 00:02:41,500
Ruth Adeyemi: By then half of it's covered in sand.

40
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:49,500
Priya Raman: And what happens to whatever's written on the paper while it's sitting in the tray?

41
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,550
Ruth Adeyemi: Well, that's the problem.

42
00:02:55,650 --> 00:03:01,200
Ruth Adeyemi: First shift finds something, a leak, a noise, whatever, and they write it on the back of a work order.

43
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:06,850
Ruth Adeyemi: Second shift never sees it.

44
00:03:06,950 --> 00:03:12,500
Ruth Adeyemi: Second shift gets their own stack at three, and whatever first shift found is sitting in a tray by the office.

45
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:20,500
Priya Raman: So second shift walks the floor without anything first shift learned that day.

46
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:23,100
Ruth Adeyemi: Right.

47
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:25,300
Ruth Adeyemi: And it's not anybody's fault.

48
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:27,500
Ruth Adeyemi: It's just paper.

49
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:32,500
Priya Raman: Can you think of a time this really cost you?

50
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,667
Ruth Adeyemi: Yeah.

51
00:03:36,767 --> 00:03:40,433
Ruth Adeyemi: Last month.

52
00:03:40,533 --> 00:03:44,200
Ruth Adeyemi: Our sand mixer at the main plant went down for two full shifts.

53
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:47,967
Ruth Adeyemi: And when we pieced it together after, one of my first shift guys, Marco, had heard the main bearing grinding two days before.

54
00:03:48,067 --> 00:03:51,733
Ruth Adeyemi: He mentioned it to the guy next to him.

55
00:03:51,833 --> 00:03:55,500
Ruth Adeyemi: He never wrote it up.

56
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,500
Priya Raman: So two days of warning, and it lived in one conversation.

57
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,100
Ruth Adeyemi: Yep.

58
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:10,300
Ruth Adeyemi: And the mixer feeds the whole molding line.

59
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:14,500
Ruth Adeyemi: So when it's down, we're not making molds, and if we're not making molds, we're not pouring.

60
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:21,500
Priya Raman: What did those two shifts look like on the floor?

61
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:28,433
Ruth Adeyemi: Pretty ugly.

62
00:04:28,533 --> 00:04:34,967
Ruth Adeyemi: The molding crew stood around for the first couple of hours, then we sent some people home.

63
00:04:35,067 --> 00:04:41,500
Ruth Adeyemi: And then we ran a Saturday to catch up on a customer order, and that customer still called me twice that week.

64
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,500
Priya Raman: That's a rough week.

65
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,433
Ruth Adeyemi: It was.

66
00:04:49,533 --> 00:04:53,967
Ruth Adeyemi: And, you know, it's not like we haven't tried to fix this.

67
00:04:54,067 --> 00:04:58,500
Ruth Adeyemi: We looked at a couple of other tools last spring, and it just kind of went nowhere.

68
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,200
Priya Raman: Okay.

69
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:05,500
Priya Raman: So when the mixer went down, who found out first?

70
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Ruth Adeyemi: The operator.

71
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:14,100
Ruth Adeyemi: It seized right at the start of second shift.

72
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:18,200
Ruth Adeyemi: He called Dale, who runs maintenance on second shift, and Dale called me at home.

73
00:05:18,300 --> 00:05:22,300
Ruth Adeyemi: Dale's been with us twenty-two years.

74
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:26,400
Ruth Adeyemi: He's my best guy at night.

75
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:30,500
Ruth Adeyemi: Second shift runs three to eleven-thirty, and most nights he's the only maintenance lead in the building.

76
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:37,500
Priya Raman: Have you put a number on what the mixer cost you?

77
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:43,700
Ruth Adeyemi: I had our controller run it after.

78
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:49,500
Ruth Adeyemi: Between the castings we scrapped and the Saturday overtime, it came to about $38,000.

79
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,500
Priya Raman: $38,000 from one bearing.

80
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,433
Ruth Adeyemi: From a bearing that costs maybe four hundred dollars.

81
00:05:57,533 --> 00:06:01,967
Ruth Adeyemi: That's the part that gets me.

82
00:06:02,067 --> 00:06:06,500
Ruth Adeyemi: I keep doing that math in my head, and it doesn't get any better.

83
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:11,500
Priya Raman: How did that number land with people?

84
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,767
Ruth Adeyemi: Sandra, our GM, asked me how we didn't see it coming.

85
00:06:16,867 --> 00:06:21,633
Ruth Adeyemi: And the honest answer was, we did see it coming.

86
00:06:21,733 --> 00:06:26,500
Ruth Adeyemi: We just didn't write it down anywhere anybody could find it.

87
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:33,500
Priya Raman: That's a hard answer to have to give.

88
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,522
Ruth Adeyemi: It was.

89
00:06:37,622 --> 00:06:41,144
Ruth Adeyemi: I don't want to give it again.

90
00:06:41,244 --> 00:06:44,767
Ruth Adeyemi: And it's not just the mixer, that's just the one that got expensive enough for Sandra to hear about.

91
00:06:44,867 --> 00:06:48,389
Ruth Adeyemi: Somebody said the shakeout conveyor sounded funny last week.

92
00:06:48,489 --> 00:06:52,011
Ruth Adeyemi: They all get mentioned.

93
00:06:52,111 --> 00:06:55,633
Ruth Adeyemi: None of them get written down.

94
00:06:55,733 --> 00:06:59,256
Ruth Adeyemi: And our second plant's worse, honestly.

95
00:06:59,356 --> 00:07:02,878
Ruth Adeyemi: It's smaller, there's no planner over there, and the lead keeps everything on a clipboard he carries around.

96
00:07:02,978 --> 00:07:06,500
Ruth Adeyemi: If he's out sick, nobody knows what's due.

97
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:14,500
Priya Raman: Can I ask what made you reach out to us now, and not, say, last year?

98
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:18,800
Ruth Adeyemi: The mixer, mostly.

99
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:22,700
Ruth Adeyemi: Sandra asking me that question, really.

100
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:26,600
Ruth Adeyemi: And then I was on your website and saw the part about techs closing work orders from their phones.

101
00:07:26,700 --> 00:07:30,500
Ruth Adeyemi: That's what made me book this.

102
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:35,500
Priya Raman: What about it caught your eye?

103
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:41,700
Ruth Adeyemi: I keep picturing it, honestly.

104
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:47,500
Ruth Adeyemi: So if Dale on second shift could see first shift's note on his phone before he walks the floor...

105
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,200
Priya Raman: Keep going.

106
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:56,500
Priya Raman: Walk me through what Dale would do with that at eleven at night.

107
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:00,856
Ruth Adeyemi: Okay.

108
00:08:00,956 --> 00:08:04,811
Ruth Adeyemi: So eleven at night is when it matters, because by then it's just Dale.

109
00:08:04,911 --> 00:08:08,767
Ruth Adeyemi: First shift's gone home, I'm asleep, there's nobody to call.

110
00:08:08,867 --> 00:08:12,722
Ruth Adeyemi: If something sounds wrong, he's deciding by himself whether to shut a machine down.

111
00:08:12,822 --> 00:08:16,678
Ruth Adeyemi: So he's walking past the mixer and it sounds a little off.

112
00:08:16,778 --> 00:08:20,633
Ruth Adeyemi: He pulls out his phone, he looks up the mixer, and he sees what Marco wrote that morning.

113
00:08:20,733 --> 00:08:24,589
Ruth Adeyemi: Main bearing grinding, check it.

114
00:08:24,689 --> 00:08:28,544
Ruth Adeyemi: Now he's not guessing.

115
00:08:28,644 --> 00:08:32,500
Ruth Adeyemi: Two guys heard the same thing.

116
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:36,500
Priya Raman: And then what does he do?

117
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:43,550
Ruth Adeyemi: He puts his hand on the housing.

118
00:08:43,650 --> 00:08:50,200
Ruth Adeyemi: If it's hot, he waits for the end of the batch, shuts it down and swaps the bearing.

119
00:08:50,300 --> 00:08:56,850
Ruth Adeyemi: Forty minutes, maybe an hour, and molding barely notices.

120
00:08:56,950 --> 00:09:03,500
Ruth Adeyemi: And then he writes down that he swapped it, so first shift sees it at six in the morning and doesn't tear it apart again looking for the noise.

121
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,500
Priya Raman: So the note goes both ways.

122
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:14,767
Ruth Adeyemi: It has to.

123
00:09:14,867 --> 00:09:20,633
Ruth Adeyemi: Right now first shift doesn't know what second shift did either.

124
00:09:20,733 --> 00:09:26,500
Ruth Adeyemi: They find a new bearing in there and nobody knows why, or who put it in, or whether it was the right one.

125
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:34,500
Priya Raman: And what would it mean for Dale to have that?

126
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
Ruth Adeyemi: Honestly?

127
00:09:38,100 --> 00:09:41,100
Ruth Adeyemi: He'd sleep better.

128
00:09:41,200 --> 00:09:44,200
Ruth Adeyemi: He hates calling me at home.

129
00:09:44,300 --> 00:09:47,300
Ruth Adeyemi: He thinks it makes him look like he can't handle it, and he can handle it.

130
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:50,400
Ruth Adeyemi: He just doesn't have the information.

131
00:09:50,500 --> 00:09:53,500
Ruth Adeyemi: He's making a call at eleven at night with half the story.

132
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:59,986
Priya Raman: Let me read that back in your words, so I know I've got it.

133
00:10:00,086 --> 00:10:06,071
Priya Raman: It's eleven at night and Dale's by himself, with nobody to call.

134
00:10:06,171 --> 00:10:12,157
Priya Raman: He walks past the mixer, it sounds off, he pulls it up on his phone and sees Marco's note from that morning, main bearing grinding, check it.

135
00:10:12,257 --> 00:10:18,243
Priya Raman: So he's not guessing.

136
00:10:18,343 --> 00:10:24,329
Priya Raman: He feels the housing, swaps the bearing at the end of the batch, and writes it down so first shift sees it at six.

137
00:10:24,429 --> 00:10:30,414
Priya Raman: He's not making the call with half the story, and he doesn't have to call you at home.

138
00:10:30,514 --> 00:10:36,500
Priya Raman: Is that it?

139
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:38,767
Ruth Adeyemi: That's it.

140
00:10:38,867 --> 00:10:40,633
Ruth Adeyemi: That's exactly it.

141
00:10:40,733 --> 00:10:42,500
Ruth Adeyemi: That's really the whole thing.

142
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:47,700
Priya Raman: Can I tell you about one of our customers?

143
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:52,500
Priya Raman: I think parts of it will sound familiar.

144
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,500
Ruth Adeyemi: Sure, go ahead.

145
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:01,986
Priya Raman: They're a die-casting company in Indiana, two plants, a little bigger than you.

146
00:11:02,086 --> 00:11:07,071
Priya Raman: When I first met their operations director, she told me almost the same story.

147
00:11:07,171 --> 00:11:12,157
Priya Raman: First shift would find something, and second shift would never hear about it.

148
00:11:12,257 --> 00:11:17,243
Priya Raman: Their night lead had a binder, and the binder was always a day behind.

149
00:11:17,343 --> 00:11:22,329
Priya Raman: Then they lost a trim press for most of a weekend, and it turned out a day shift tech had flagged a leak on it that Tuesday.

150
00:11:22,429 --> 00:11:27,414
Priya Raman: On paper.

151
00:11:27,514 --> 00:11:32,500
Priya Raman: In the tray.

152
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,500
Ruth Adeyemi: Oh, that's us.

153
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:42,620
Priya Raman: So the first thing they did with us wasn't the whole schedule.

154
00:11:42,720 --> 00:11:49,340
Priya Raman: It was just the notes.

155
00:11:49,440 --> 00:11:56,060
Priya Raman: Every tech could put a note on a machine from their phone, and the next shift saw it before they walked the floor.

156
00:11:56,160 --> 00:12:02,780
Priya Raman: We didn't touch their PM schedule for the first month.

157
00:12:02,880 --> 00:12:09,500
Priya Raman: Within a couple of months, their night lead was catching two or three things a week that used to wait until morning, and the schedule came after, once people trusted it.

158
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,433
Ruth Adeyemi: Two or three a week.

159
00:12:12,533 --> 00:12:14,967
Ruth Adeyemi: Huh.

160
00:12:15,067 --> 00:12:17,500
Ruth Adeyemi: That's more than we catch in a month.

161
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,620
Priya Raman: And the reason I bring them up is something you said earlier.

162
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:25,340
Priya Raman: You said second shift never sees what first shift found.

163
00:12:25,440 --> 00:12:29,060
Priya Raman: That's exactly where they started.

164
00:12:29,160 --> 00:12:32,780
Priya Raman: Does that ring true for Harrow Creek?

165
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:36,500
Priya Raman: Or is something different for you?

166
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:40,843
Ruth Adeyemi: No, it rings true.

167
00:12:40,943 --> 00:12:44,786
Ruth Adeyemi: The one thing that's different is the guys.

168
00:12:44,886 --> 00:12:48,729
Ruth Adeyemi: A lot of my crew has been here twenty, thirty years.

169
00:12:48,829 --> 00:12:52,671
Ruth Adeyemi: Some of them still have flip phones in their lockers.

170
00:12:52,771 --> 00:12:56,614
Ruth Adeyemi: That's what I meant at the start about guys who don't sit at a desk.

171
00:12:56,714 --> 00:13:00,557
Ruth Adeyemi: I can see Dale using it.

172
00:13:00,657 --> 00:13:04,500
Ruth Adeyemi: I'm less sure about some of the others.

173
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:09,557
Priya Raman: That's a real concern, and I'm glad you said it.

174
00:13:09,657 --> 00:13:14,214
Priya Raman: At that die-caster, the person most against it was their night lead.

175
00:13:14,314 --> 00:13:18,871
Priya Raman: Twenty-eight years in.

176
00:13:18,971 --> 00:13:23,529
Priya Raman: What turned him was that closing a work order took him under a minute, standing right at the machine.

177
00:13:23,629 --> 00:13:28,186
Priya Raman: He didn't have to walk anywhere or type much.

178
00:13:28,286 --> 00:13:32,843
Priya Raman: It took him about two weeks.

179
00:13:32,943 --> 00:13:37,500
Priya Raman: By the end of the first month, he was the one telling the younger guys to put their notes in.

180
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:39,700
Ruth Adeyemi: Wait, back up.

181
00:13:39,800 --> 00:13:41,500
Ruth Adeyemi: How does he not type much?

182
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:48,433
Priya Raman: He'd snap a photo of whatever he found and add a few words.

183
00:13:48,533 --> 00:13:54,967
Priya Raman: A lot of the older guys like that better than typing.

184
00:13:55,067 --> 00:14:01,500
Priya Raman: They also put a shared phone at each maintenance station for anybody who didn't want it on their own phone.

185
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:05,050
Ruth Adeyemi: Okay.

186
00:14:05,150 --> 00:14:08,200
Ruth Adeyemi: The shared phone thing would matter.

187
00:14:08,300 --> 00:14:11,350
Ruth Adeyemi: Some of my guys won't put a work app on their personal phone, period.

188
00:14:11,450 --> 00:14:14,500
Ruth Adeyemi: And I don't blame them.

189
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:20,100
Priya Raman: That's really common.

190
00:14:20,200 --> 00:14:25,300
Priya Raman: And for your second plant, it's one schedule across both.

191
00:14:25,400 --> 00:14:30,500
Priya Raman: The lead with the clipboard would see the same thing you see, and you'd see his, without driving over.

192
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:35,300
Ruth Adeyemi: That alone would help.

193
00:14:35,400 --> 00:14:39,700
Ruth Adeyemi: I drive over there twice a week just to find out what's going on.

194
00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:44,100
Ruth Adeyemi: It's forty minutes each way.

195
00:14:44,200 --> 00:14:48,500
Ruth Adeyemi: And then I'm back here by three trying to catch second shift.

196
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:55,500
Priya Raman: Can I ask how a decision like this gets made at Harrow Creek?

197
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:00,220
Ruth Adeyemi: So I'd bring it.

198
00:15:00,320 --> 00:15:04,540
Ruth Adeyemi: But really it comes down to two people.

199
00:15:04,640 --> 00:15:08,860
Ruth Adeyemi: Walt Brenner is our plant manager at the main plant, and he has to believe the crew will actually use it.

200
00:15:08,960 --> 00:15:13,180
Ruth Adeyemi: Because if Walt doesn't believe it, the crew won't either.

201
00:15:13,280 --> 00:15:17,500
Ruth Adeyemi: And then Sandra Kim, our general manager, signs.

202
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:22,500
Priya Raman: And what will matter most to Sandra?

203
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:29,500
Ruth Adeyemi: She'll ask if this is another system the guys won't use.

204
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:37,500
Priya Raman: What would she need to see to believe they will?

205
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:41,300
Ruth Adeyemi: Walt telling her.

206
00:15:41,400 --> 00:15:44,700
Ruth Adeyemi: If Walt says the guys will use it, she'll believe it.

207
00:15:44,800 --> 00:15:48,100
Ruth Adeyemi: She won't really believe me, because I'm the one who wants it.

208
00:15:48,200 --> 00:15:51,500
Ruth Adeyemi: And she, um, definitely won't believe you.

209
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:53,767
Priya Raman: That's fair.

210
00:15:53,867 --> 00:15:55,633
Priya Raman: I wouldn't either.

211
00:15:55,733 --> 00:15:57,500
Priya Raman: What does Walt care about?

212
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:03,020
Ruth Adeyemi: Walt cares about not adding work for his guys.

213
00:16:03,120 --> 00:16:08,140
Ruth Adeyemi: If it's one more thing to fill out, he'll kill it.

214
00:16:08,240 --> 00:16:13,260
Ruth Adeyemi: He's not a no person.

215
00:16:13,360 --> 00:16:18,380
Ruth Adeyemi: He just protects his crew.

216
00:16:18,480 --> 00:16:23,500
Ruth Adeyemi: He ran second shift maintenance here for eight years before he was plant manager, so he knows what it's like when somebody in an office hands you one more form.

217
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:28,200
Priya Raman: That sounds like a good plant manager.

218
00:16:28,300 --> 00:16:32,500
Priya Raman: Has Walt heard about any of this yet?

219
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:36,050
Ruth Adeyemi: A little.

220
00:16:36,150 --> 00:16:39,200
Ruth Adeyemi: He knows about the mixer and Marco, obviously.

221
00:16:39,300 --> 00:16:42,350
Ruth Adeyemi: He was there for the whole thing.

222
00:16:42,450 --> 00:16:45,500
Ruth Adeyemi: I haven't told him I'm talking to anybody yet.

223
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:49,700
Priya Raman: Is there anyone else who'd weigh in?

224
00:16:49,800 --> 00:16:53,500
Priya Raman: IT, finance, your planner?

225
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:58,550
Ruth Adeyemi: Our IT guy will want to know what it connects to and where the data lives, but he won't stop it.

226
00:16:58,650 --> 00:17:03,200
Ruth Adeyemi: Our planner will want to know it doesn't make her day longer.

227
00:17:03,300 --> 00:17:07,850
Ruth Adeyemi: And Sandra handles the money side herself.

228
00:17:07,950 --> 00:17:12,500
Ruth Adeyemi: We're not that big.

229
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:16,500
Priya Raman: Is there a timing you're working toward?

230
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:22,800
Ruth Adeyemi: Sandra sets next year's budget in November.

231
00:17:22,900 --> 00:17:28,700
Ruth Adeyemi: She does it the week before Thanksgiving, every year, and once it's set, it's set.

232
00:17:28,800 --> 00:17:34,600
Ruth Adeyemi: So if this is going to happen in the first quarter, she needs to see it before then.

233
00:17:34,700 --> 00:17:40,500
Ruth Adeyemi: Otherwise it waits a year, and I'm not doing another year of the tray.

234
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:46,433
Priya Raman: Okay.

235
00:17:46,533 --> 00:17:51,967
Priya Raman: So it's Walt believing the crew will use it, a quick look from IT, your planner seeing it saves her time, and Sandra signing before her budget's set in November.

236
00:17:52,067 --> 00:17:57,500
Priya Raman: Did I get that right?

237
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,700
Ruth Adeyemi: Yeah, that's right.

238
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:03,500
Ruth Adeyemi: That's the whole list.

239
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:10,700
Priya Raman: Would Walt and Sandra be willing to join the next conversation?

240
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:17,500
Priya Raman: Honestly, I think it would go better than me trying to reach them through you.

241
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,550
Ruth Adeyemi: Walt, yes.

242
00:18:20,650 --> 00:18:23,200
Ruth Adeyemi: Sandra, maybe, if it's short.

243
00:18:23,300 --> 00:18:25,850
Ruth Adeyemi: She hates long vendor calls.

244
00:18:25,950 --> 00:18:28,500
Ruth Adeyemi: The last one went ninety minutes and she still brings it up.

245
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:33,433
Priya Raman: Thirty minutes.

246
00:18:33,533 --> 00:18:37,967
Priya Raman: And I'd want Walt doing most of the talking, not me.

247
00:18:38,067 --> 00:18:42,500
Priya Raman: What would Walt need to see in that half hour to believe his crew would use it?

248
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:48,767
Ruth Adeyemi: Honestly, a tech closing a work order on a phone.

249
00:18:48,867 --> 00:18:54,633
Ruth Adeyemi: If he can hand the phone to one of his own guys and watch him do it in under a minute, he'll get it.

250
00:18:54,733 --> 00:19:00,500
Ruth Adeyemi: Can you set that up?

251
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:06,767
Priya Raman: Yes.

252
00:19:06,867 --> 00:19:12,633
Priya Raman: We'll set up a demo with your mixer and a few of your machines in it, so it's your plant on the screen and not a made-up one.

253
00:19:12,733 --> 00:19:18,500
Priya Raman: He can hand the phone to whoever he wants.

254
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:22,100
Ruth Adeyemi: Then Sandra will come.

255
00:19:22,200 --> 00:19:25,300
Ruth Adeyemi: And Walt will like that a lot.

256
00:19:25,400 --> 00:19:28,500
Ruth Adeyemi: He'll probably grab Dale for it.

257
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:34,820
Priya Raman: Great.

258
00:19:34,920 --> 00:19:40,740
Priya Raman: Then let's get it on the calendar while we're both here, so it doesn't turn into a week of email.

259
00:19:40,840 --> 00:19:46,660
Priya Raman: I have Tuesday the 13th at 10 or Wednesday the 14th at 2, Eastern.

260
00:19:46,760 --> 00:19:52,580
Priya Raman: And the reason I'd push for it is so Sandra hears it from Walt, not from a vendor.

261
00:19:52,680 --> 00:19:58,500
Priya Raman: Walt sees the phone side, asks his own questions, and Sandra hears what he thinks well before November.

262
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:02,200
Ruth Adeyemi: Okay, that makes sense.

263
00:20:02,300 --> 00:20:05,500
Ruth Adeyemi: Hang on, let me pull up my calendar.

264
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:15,500
Priya Raman: Take your time.

265
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:19,667
Ruth Adeyemi: Okay.

266
00:20:19,767 --> 00:20:23,433
Ruth Adeyemi: Tuesday the 13th is no good, Walt has a customer audit that morning.

267
00:20:23,533 --> 00:20:27,200
Ruth Adeyemi: Wednesday the 14th at 2 works for me, and it looks like Walt's open.

268
00:20:27,300 --> 00:20:30,967
Ruth Adeyemi: Sandra's showing free too.

269
00:20:31,067 --> 00:20:34,733
Ruth Adeyemi: Let's do Wednesday the 14th at 2.

270
00:20:34,833 --> 00:20:38,500
Ruth Adeyemi: Their emails are the same as mine, first initial and last name.

271
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:42,800
Priya Raman: Got it.

272
00:20:42,900 --> 00:20:46,700
Priya Raman: I'm sending it now to all three of you, Wednesday the 14th, 2 to 2:30 Eastern.

273
00:20:46,800 --> 00:20:50,600
Priya Raman: Okay, it's sent.

274
00:20:50,700 --> 00:20:54,500
Priya Raman: Did it come through on your end?

275
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:58,300
Ruth Adeyemi: Hang on.

276
00:20:58,400 --> 00:21:01,700
Ruth Adeyemi: Yep, it's here.

277
00:21:01,800 --> 00:21:05,100
Ruth Adeyemi: I'm accepting it now.

278
00:21:05,200 --> 00:21:08,500
Ruth Adeyemi: And I'll walk over and tell Walt in person, so it doesn't just show up on his calendar.

279
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:12,667
Priya Raman: Good.

280
00:21:12,767 --> 00:21:16,433
Priya Raman: In the description I put one line, a tech closes a work order on a phone in under a minute, in front of Walt.

281
00:21:16,533 --> 00:21:20,200
Priya Raman: If he wants anything else on there, he can just reply to the invite.

282
00:21:20,300 --> 00:21:23,967
Priya Raman: And here's my cell, in case anything comes up before then.

283
00:21:24,067 --> 00:21:27,733
Priya Raman: It's 312-555-0142.

284
00:21:27,833 --> 00:21:31,500
Priya Raman: Texting is easiest.

285
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:37,700
Ruth Adeyemi: Got it, putting it in my phone now.

286
00:21:37,800 --> 00:21:43,500
Ruth Adeyemi: And I'll send you a list of what's on the floor at the main plant, so the demo looks like us.

287
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:47,433
Priya Raman: Thanks, Ruth.

288
00:21:47,533 --> 00:21:50,967
Priya Raman: This was really helpful.

289
00:21:51,067 --> 00:21:54,500
Priya Raman: And tell Dale I said hi, even though he doesn't know who I am yet.

290
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:56,767
Ruth Adeyemi: Ha.

291
00:21:56,867 --> 00:21:58,633
Ruth Adeyemi: I will.

292
00:21:58,733 --> 00:22:00,500
Ruth Adeyemi: Talk to you on the 14th.

293
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:02,900
Priya Raman: Talk then.

294
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:04,900
Priya Raman: Bye.
